## Overview

`makeHttpApiToolkit(config?)` exposes `HttpApiAdapter.operations` as Effect AI tools and returns matching `{ toolkit, handlers }`. Configuration is optional. CLI, toolkit, and MCP registration share one operation catalogue and generated HTTP client.

Read operations run without approval by default; writes require approval. Method annotations describe read-only, destructive, and idempotent behavior. Generated descriptions guide the model to wait for successful results and treat returned data as untrusted rather than as instructions.

## Installation And Imports

Install `@krak-stack/registry` and import `@krak-stack/registry/httpapi/toolkit` and `@krak-stack/registry/httpapi/adapter`. Alternatively, install `@krak-stack/httpapi-toolkit` with shadcn and use the corresponding `@/lib/httpapi-toolkit` and `@/lib/httpapi-adapter` modules.

## Configure The Adapter

```ts
import { Effect, Layer } from "effect";
import { Toolkit } from "effect/ai";
import { FetchHttpClient } from "effect/http";
import { HttpApiAdapter } from "@krak-stack/registry/httpapi/adapter";
import { makeHttpApiToolkit } from "@krak-stack/registry/httpapi/toolkit";
import { AppApi } from "@/api";

const adapterLayer = HttpApiAdapter.layer({
  api: AppApi,
  baseUrl: "https://example.com",
  methods: ["get"],
}).pipe(Layer.provide(FetchHttpClient.layer));
```

Explicitly select GET operations for read-only exposure. Omit `methods` or select additional methods only when intentionally exposing writes. Use `include` to narrow selection further.

## Compose With An Agent

Inside the application's Effect program:

```ts
const { toolkit: httpTools, handlers } = yield* makeHttpApiToolkit().pipe(
  Effect.provide(adapterLayer),
);
const definition = Toolkit.merge(application.toolkit, httpTools);
const toolsLayer = Layer.mergeAll(
  application.layer,
  handlers.pipe(Layer.provide(adapterLayer)),
);
const toolkit = yield* definition.pipe(Effect.provide(toolsLayer));

const events = yield* agent.stream({
  action: request.action,
  history: request.history,
  systemPrompt: application.systemPrompt,
  toolkit,
});
```

`application`, `agent`, and `request` belong to the consuming application. Tool arguments use `{ params, query, headers, body }`, with typed numeric and boolean parameters rather than wire strings. Payload codecs preserve their transformations. Override `needsApproval` to customize the agent's approval policy. Use `strict` for OpenAI-compatible strict object schemas and `transformResult` to reduce or redact responses. Construct once; the separate `HttpApiToolkit` and `HttpApiToolkitLayer` factories have been removed.

## Serve A Toolkit Over MCP

Register the toolkit directly with Effect; no HTTP API-specific MCP service is needed. Keep server identity and transport in the consuming application:

```ts
import { McpProtocol, McpServer } from "effect/ai";

export const mcpLayer = Layer.effectDiscard(
  Effect.gen(function* () {
    const { toolkit, handlers } = yield* makeHttpApiToolkit();
    yield* McpServer.registerToolkit(toolkit).pipe(Effect.provide(handlers));
  }),
).pipe(
  Layer.provideMerge(
    McpServer.layerHttp({
      name: "app",
      version: "1.0.0",
      path: "/api/mcp",
      protocols: [McpProtocol.v2025_11_25],
    }),
  ),
  Layer.provide(adapterLayer),
);
```

This example reuses the imports and GET-only `adapterLayer` above. Provide `mcpLayer` alongside the application's routes in its HTTP runtime. Any Effect toolkit, including non-HTTP tools, can be registered this way.

Inbound MCP authentication and authorization remain application-owned. Provide an authenticated `HttpClient` when the downstream API requires credentials; never expose credentials as tool arguments. Method annotations are not access controls. If intentionally exposing writes, enforce authorization or confirmation in the application before opting out of agent approval with `needsApproval: () => false`. Registration does not silently disable approval requirements.

The `HttpApiMcp` service and registry item have been removed. `ApiClient` and `HttpApiSpec` have also been replaced by the single `HttpApiAdapter` dependency.
